= Miso

_Scope: This entry treats_ miso _(味噌) — the canonical Japanese fermented soybean-and-grain paste, the foundational seasoning of Japanese cuisine. Miso is a_ post-classical fermented food _from the East Asian perspective: it derives from the ancient Chinese_ 醬 (jiàng) _fermented-paste tradition (Zhou dynasty onwards, attested in the_ Zhouli _and Confucius's_ Analects_) and was introduced to Japan in the 7th c. via Buddhist monks, where it was elaborated into the distinct Japanese forms. Status: not a canonical Bencao_ drug _entry but a substantial Chinese dietetic-culinary category (醬); classical Japanese dietary register; not classical in Ayurveda or Tibb; modern macrobiotic re-emergence (Ohsawa, Kushi) as a "healing food"; well-documented modern nutrition with substantive sodium and probiotic considerations. Closely related to_ shoyu _(醤油) — see_ shoyu.typ_._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* Miso is *not a plant* — it is a *fermented food product* made from *_Glycine max_* (L.) Merr. (soybean, Fabaceae), often combined with grain (rice _Oryza sativa_ or barley _Hordeum vulgare_), *salt*, and a *kōji starter* — _Aspergillus oryzae_ Ahlb. (the koji mold, the same organism that ferments sake and shoyu). Modern food-science taxonomy treats miso as a category of *moromi-type* fermented bean-paste foods, alongside Korean *doenjang* and Chinese *douban jiang*.

*Japanese:* *味噌 (miso)* — Modern Japanese; the characters 味 mi ("flavor, taste") + 噌 so ("an obscure character used for this word specifically"). The earliest Japanese attestations (Nara period, 8th c.) use the writing *未醤 (mishō)*, "unripe sauce" — preserving the historical understanding of miso as an *intermediate* (unfully-ripened) state of the Chinese 醬 jiàng-class paste. By the Heian period (794–1185) the modern 味噌 spelling was established. The Japanese refinement of the Chinese 醬 into the distinctive Japanese forms is a multi-century process; by the Kamakura period (1185–1333) miso was an established staple, and by the Edo period (1603–1868) the regional types — *shiro miso* (Kyoto-style white, sweet), *aka miso* (Tohoku and central red, salty, long-fermented), *hatcho miso* (Aichi, soybean-only, no grain), *saikyo miso* (Kyoto sweet white), *genmai miso* (brown rice), *mugi miso* (barley, Kyushu) — were established.#footnote[Etymology and history: standard Japanese food-history literature; the Nara-period 未醤 attestation and the Heian-period 味噌 standardization are documented in food-historical scholarship (e.g. Shurtleff & Aoyagi, _The Book of Miso_, 2nd ed., Soyinfo Center, 2001). The Buddhist-monk transmission of fermented-paste technology from Tang China to Nara Japan is the canonical history.]

*Chinese:* *醬 (jiàng)* — the *parent category* of fermented bean-and-grain pastes, ancient in classical China; attestation in the *Zhouli* (周禮, "Rites of Zhou," Warring States — Han period), *Lunyu* (論語, Confucius's _Analects_ — *10.8*: "He did not eat what was not properly seasoned with sauce [醬]"); central in classical Chinese dietetics. The Chinese 醬 tradition is *not specifically a Bencao drug* — it is a *foundational food category* rather than a pharmacopoeial entry, though related fermented soybean products *do appear in the Bencao* (especially *豆豉 (dòuchǐ)*, fermented black soybeans, which *is* a canonical Bencao entry and a Han-dynasty Shanghan-Lun drug). The closest Chinese analogue to Japanese miso is *豆瓣醬 (dòubàn jiàng)* — Sichuanese fermented broad bean paste — though this is a different fermentation profile. The modern Chinese term for Japanese miso is *味噌 (wèicēng)* — a direct kanji-borrowing.

*Korean:* *된장 (doenjang)* — the Korean parallel fermented soybean paste; *similar but distinct* from miso (longer fermentation, no koji-mold starter in the canonical preparation, more intense flavor); both descend from the Chinese 醬 tradition via parallel adaptation. Korean *간장 (ganjang)* is the parallel of shoyu.

*Sanskrit / Arabic / Latin classical:* *Not classical in Ayurveda, Tibb, or Greco-Roman traditions.* Miso is an *East Asian fermented food* that did not enter the western or South Asian classical pharmacopoeias. Modern Indian Ayurvedic and Western reasoning about miso is *post-classical extension* only.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*Miso itself has no classical Chinese Bencao entry* — it is a Japanese refinement of the Chinese 醬 tradition, post-dating the canonical 本草. However, *the parent category 醬* (and its closely-related fermented soybean drug *豆豉 dòuchǐ*) *are classical Chinese*, and the reading of miso *by extension* on the classical 醬 / 豆豉 axes is well-founded.

*Parent category — 醬 (jiàng) as a dietetic class:* the Chinese classical dietetic literature treats 醬 as a *warming, salty-pungent, fermented condiment* used in moderate amounts to *harmonize the Stomach and stimulate appetite*. Confucius's 10.8 register is dietary-prescriptive: 醬 is essential to proper meal preparation, with specific 醬 paired to specific dishes (the classical "matching" doctrine).

*豆豉 (dòuchǐ, fermented black soybeans) — the closest canonical Bencao analog:* warm (溫), pungent-bitter (辛苦), Lung-Stomach (肺胃) channels; actions *解表除煩 (releases surface, eliminates vexation)* — used in the Han-dynasty *梔子豉湯 (Zhī Zǐ Chǐ Tāng)* of the *Shanghan Lun* for residual heat in the chest after surface-disease resolution. The fermented soybean register is canonical Chinese.

*Miso reading by extension:* on the parent 醬 and 豆豉 axes, miso reads as:
- *Property:* warm (溫) — close to slightly warm; varies by type (white short-fermented shiro miso closer to neutral; long-fermented red aka miso clearly warming).
- *Flavor:* salty (鹹), sweet (甘 — for the koji-fermented sweetness), with bitter (苦) and umami notes.
- *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Kidney (腎) — the Spleen-Stomach for the digestive register; Kidney for the deep-salty register.
- *Actions:* *和胃 (hé wèi, harmonizes Stomach)*, *補中 (bǔ zhōng, tonifies the middle)*, *化濕 (huà shī, transforms damp)*, *解毒 (jiě dú, resolves toxin — the modern macrobiotic register but also classical in some accounts)*.
- *Cautions:* damp-heat patterns; sodium concern in hypertension; food stagnation with heavy use.#footnote[醬 in Zhouli; 論語 10.8; 豆豉 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ and _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. — 淡豆豉 entry. The reading of Japanese miso by extension on the parent 醬 and 豆豉 axes is principled extrapolation, not canonical Bencao citation — flagged.]

*Modern macrobiotic Chinese-medicine register:* the 20th-century macrobiotic movement (George Ohsawa, Michio Kushi) elaborates miso as a *yang, warming, protective food* — particularly in relation to the post-Hiroshima Japanese folk-medical reports of miso-soup-drinkers having reduced radiation effects (the Tatsuichirō Akizuki / St. Francis Hospital story, Nagasaki 1945). This register is post-classical, often hyperbolic, but has cultural significance.

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Miso is not classical in Ayurveda._ Soybeans themselves are not classical Indian dravyas (soybean is an East Asian crop, post-classical in Indian dietetics); fermented soybean preparations have no Sanskrit name in the canonical literature. The reading below applies dravyaguna principles to miso *by extension only* — heavily flagged, with the recognition that the substance is *outside the classical Indian foodscape*.

*Rasa (taste):* Lavaṇa (लवण, salty) — predominant; Madhura (मधुर, sweet); Tikta (तिक्त, bitter — mild); the umami fifth-taste category (not a classical Indian rasa) is also strongly present.

*Guṇa (qualities):* Guru (गुरु, heavy), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous), Sāndra (सान्द्र, dense), warm-aromatic from fermentation.

*Vīrya (potency):* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, heating) for the long-fermented dark types; near-neutral for the short-fermented sweet white types. The koji-mold-fermentation register produces a heating effect comparable to other long-fermented foods (sauerkraut, kimchi).

*Vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet) — the long-fermented forms have a sweet vipāka register characteristic of well-fermented protein-rich foods.

*Prabhāva:* No prabhāva possible — no classical entry. The fermentation-mediated probiotic effect would be a modern interpretive prabhāva candidate.

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Vāta (snigdha-salty-warming) and Kapha (the fermentation-warming-and-drying register); aggravates Pitta at heavy intake (the high salt, the warming fermentation).

*Karma (by extension):*
- _Dīpana, pācana_ — kindles digestion (the umami / glutamate effect; the probiotic register).
- _Vāta-anulomana_ — relieves Vāta downward.
- _Balya_ — strength-giving (the soybean protein register).
- _Used in (modern reasoned context):_ digestive support, immune-supportive registers in modern Indian post-classical dietetics.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

- *(S, extended) Conditional viruddha:* Pitta excess; hypertension (modern medical caveat — sodium); kidney disease (sodium); the high-salt long-fermented forms are inappropriate in Pitta-loaded or sodium-restricted patients.
- *(M) Modern medical:*
  - *Sodium content* — *substantial* (typically ~10–15% by weight); hypertensive patients should moderate; renal disease patients should moderate.
  - *Biogenic amines* — long-fermented miso can contain tyramine and histamine; MAOI medication contraindication.
  - *Soybean / isoflavone considerations* — modest phytoestrogenic activity; relevant in some hormone-sensitive contexts but at typical dietary doses generally not significant.
  - *Allergy* — soybean allergy (and rarely koji-mold allergy).
  - *Vitamin K1* — modest; may matter for warfarin patients but the typical miso intake is small.
  - *Pregnancy* — generally safe at typical culinary intake.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Miso is not classical in Tibb._ Soybeans and Japanese fermented preparations are entirely outside the Mediterranean / Persian / Indian range of the Arabic-Islamic classical pharmacopoeia. Avicenna's _al-Qānūn_ does not include miso, soybean, or any Japanese fermented food; the herb is *post-classical in Tibb*. Modern Unani has not formally absorbed miso into the canonical _adwiya mufrada_ register; the Levantine and Maghrebi Muslim culinary tradition does not include miso classically.

For honest scholarly register: *no classical Tibb attestation; no Quranic / hadith provenance; no Tibb al-Nabawī reading possible*. The substance is outside the canonical reach of the tradition and should be treated as such — not "extended" into Tibb by analogy.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

Miso does not appear in any classical Chinese formula. The closely-related canonical 豆豉 (fermented black soybeans) appears in *梔子豉湯 (Zhī Zǐ Chǐ Tāng)* of the *Shanghan Lun*, where it pairs with gardenia for residual heat with vexation after febrile-disease resolution — a fascinating classical use of fermented soybeans as a *gentle releasing-and-clearing* drug. Modern Chinese cuisine uses miso (味噌 wèicēng) as an imported Japanese seasoning; no classical formula.

=== Ayurveda

No classical Sanskrit formulas use miso or soybean. Modern Indian and post-classical Ayurvedic dietetic literature includes miso as a *probiotic / digestive-supportive* food, by reasoned analogy with other fermented foods (curd, idli batter); this is *post-classical extension*, not formulary.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

No Greco-Roman or medieval European tradition. Modern Western: miso enters Western culinary and dietetic literature in the late 20th century via the macrobiotic movement (George Ohsawa's _Zen Macrobiotics_, 1965; Michio Kushi's writings) and via Japanese culinary globalization (1970s–present). Modern Western herbal and nutritional medicine treats miso as a *fermented probiotic food* with cardiovascular and digestive-supportive register; the modern Western reading is *post-classical*.

=== Japanese (the canonical home tradition)

*Miso is the foundational seasoning of Japanese cuisine* — used in:
- *Miso shiru (miso soup)* — the canonical Japanese daily breakfast and accompaniment to rice; miso paste dissolved in *dashi* (kombu-and-katsuobushi stock — see `kombu.typ`) with seasonal additions (tofu, wakame, scallion, daikon, mushrooms, leafy greens).
- *Misozuke (miso pickling)* — vegetables, fish, meat preserved in miso paste; the preservation technique extends to *gyutan (beef tongue)*, *misozuke namasu (pickled fish)*, *misozuke daikon*, and modern western applications (miso-pickled eggs, miso-cured cheese).
- *Dengaku (miso glaze)* — sweet miso glaze on grilled tofu, eggplant, daikon, fish, or skewered foods; canonical Japanese seasonal preparation.
- *Hatcho miso preparations* — the distinctive long-fermented (3+ years) soybean-only miso of Okazaki, Aichi — used in *aka dashi* miso soup, in compound seasonings, and as a long-keeping concentrated paste.
- *Saikyo miso preparations* — the sweet white Kyoto miso, used in Western Kyoto cuisine for *saikyō-yaki* (white-miso-glazed grilled fish), in winter sweets, and in compound dressings.
- *Macrobiotic register (post-1960s):* miso soup as daily yang-balancing food; specific therapeutic uses in macrobiotic clinical practice.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Miso is a *cool-temperate East Asian fermented staple* — the *cool, humid winter* of central and northern Japan is climatically apt for the long, slow miso fermentation. Hokkaido, Tohoku, central Honshu (Niigata, Nagano, the Japan Sea coast) — these are the heart of the traditional miso production region; cold winters allow the slow lactobacillus fermentation that develops the deep flavors of long-aged red and dark miso. The Kyoto sweet white miso (saikyo) reflects the warmer central Honshu register with shorter fermentation. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Continental seasonal-extreme + cool-damp (Japan, Korea, northern China)

The home climate. Cold-damp winter, hot-humid summer; constitutional swing wide across the year; the *cellar fermentation* in cool winter is *climatically apt for the long fermentations* that produce deep red and dark miso.

*Traditional preparation.* *Daily miso soup* (typically taken at breakfast, sometimes also dinner) as a *climatically calibrated warming-fermented-probiotic preparation* for the cool-damp winter and as a balancing food across the year. The pairing of miso with *kombu-katsuobushi dashi* is a constitutional-dietetic synthesis — the umami and minerals of the sea complementing the protein-and-probiotic of the fermented bean.

*Cares.* The *warming long-fermented red and dark miso* is climatically apt for *cold winter and Kapha-Vāta-dominant constitutions*; the *cooler, sweeter white miso (shiro / saikyo)* is climatically apt for *summer and Pitta-loaded constitutions*. The Japanese regional distribution preserves this climatic logic: northern (Tohoku, Niigata) tradition is dark-fermented (winter-warming); central (Kyoto, Kansai) tradition is sweet-light (warmer-climate); southern (Kyushu) tradition is mugi (barley-fermented) (warmer-climate, sweeter). *Daily heavy salt intake from miso* is moderated in summer (lighter miso, lighter soup, more vegetable additions); deeper salty preparations in winter.

=== Modern globalized use

Miso has spread globally via Japanese culinary globalization and the macrobiotic movement; modern Western and global use is *climatically agnostic*. The classical Japanese register — *deep-fermented heavy miso in cold winter; sweet-light miso in summer; daily morning miso soup as warming-tonifying food* — gives a finer constitutional reading than the modern globalized "miso as universal probiotic food" register. *In hot-humid climates, heavy daily miso soup may compound damp-heat*; in cold-damp climates the traditional Japanese register is exactly climatically apt; in Mediterranean and continental temperate climates, miso fits as a winter and shoulder-season fermented food, less central in summer.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

The macrobiotic-derived "miso as universal healing food" register has globalized daily miso intake across all climates and constitutional types; sodium load is a modern concern that the classical Japanese register implicitly moderated through seasonal adjustment. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _viruddha_ framework; see `climates.typ` for the cool-damp and continental seasonal-extreme profiles.#footnote[Climate framework: see `climates.typ`. Japanese regional miso traditions: Shurtleff & Aoyagi, _The Book of Miso_; Andoh, _Kansha_; Tsuji, _Japanese Cooking: A Simple Art_; modern Japanese food-history literature.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Approximate composition (typical aka / red miso, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~199 kcal; Water ~43 g
- Carbohydrate ~26 g; fiber ~5 g
- Protein ~12 g; Fat ~6 g
- *Sodium very high — ~3700–4500 mg per 100 g* (~10–13% by weight; *the principal modern nutritional consideration*)
- Minerals: iron ~2.5 mg, calcium ~57 mg, magnesium ~48 mg, potassium ~210 mg, manganese, zinc, copper
- Vitamins: B-vitamins (notably B12 from bacterial fermentation in some long-fermented types — though most miso has only trace B12), vitamin K2 (from bacterial fermentation — modest amount, a benefit of fermented foods), folate
- *Isoflavones* (soybean phytoestrogens) — daidzein, genistein, glycitein; the fermentation process *increases bioavailability* (converts glycoside to aglycone form); typical aka miso has 30–80 mg total isoflavones per 100 g
- *Live and dead microorganisms* — lactic-acid bacteria (Tetragenococcus halophilus, Lactobacillus species), yeasts (Zygosaccharomyces rouxii), and the residual koji mold; *traditional unpasteurized miso contains live cultures*; modern pasteurized commercial miso typically does not
- *Free amino acids* — high glutamate (the umami source), aspartate, and other amino acids released by koji proteolysis during fermentation
- *Bioactive peptides* and *melanoidins* (Maillard products from the long fermentation) — antioxidant, ACE-inhibitory, immunomodulatory#footnote[Phytochemistry and nutrition of miso: Shurtleff & Aoyagi, _The Book of Miso_; modern Japanese food-science literature; recent reviews in _Food Chemistry_, _Journal of Functional Foods_, _Nutrients_; sodium content varies substantially by miso type, with shiro (white) miso lower in salt than aka (red) miso.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Probiotic effect* — modest in unpasteurized miso; modern Japanese epidemiological studies link daily miso soup consumption to *reduced gastric cancer risk* (Tsugane et al., multiple Japanese cohort studies), *reduced cardiovascular mortality*, and modest blood-pressure effects (paradoxically *not* causing the expected hypertension despite the high sodium content — possibly due to the isoflavone, ACE-inhibitory peptide, and potassium-K profile).
- *Cardiovascular* — Japanese cohort studies suggest *neutral or modestly favorable* cardiovascular outcomes with daily miso soup, *despite the salt load*. The mechanism is debated; the most likely explanation is the *isoflavone + peptide + nutrient-density* package balancing the sodium concern. *This finding does not generalize* to ad-libitum heavy intake or to ultra-processed instant miso preparations.
- *Cancer prevention (epidemiologic)* — *reduced gastric cancer risk* with daily miso soup is a striking Japanese epidemiologic finding; *increased risk* with very heavy intake; the dose-response curve is U-shaped.
- *Isoflavone effects* — modest phytoestrogenic activity; relevant in some hormone-sensitive contexts.
- *Postbiotic effects* — even pasteurized miso retains bacterial metabolites (short-chain fatty acids, postbiotic compounds) with documented immunomodulatory activity.
- *Hiroshima-Nagasaki radiation-protection folklore* — the Tatsuichirō Akizuki / St. Francis Hospital report (Nagasaki, 1945) that miso soup may have protected hospital staff from radiation effects is *culturally significant but lacks rigorous scientific support*; the underlying mechanism (zybicolin / dipicolinic acid binding heavy metals) has some in vitro support but the clinical relevance is *not established*. Honest scholarly register: *folk-history with modest preclinical support, no rigorous clinical confirmation*.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Traditional Japanese daily intake:* 1–3 bowls of miso soup per day (~10–40 g of miso per day); the canonical Japanese pattern.
- *Sodium limit:* the principal practical limit; 1 bowl of miso soup contributes ~600–800 mg sodium; hypertensive patients should moderate.
- *Pregnancy:* generally safe at typical culinary intake; verify with obstetric guidance.
- *MAOI medication:* avoid (biogenic amines in long-fermented miso).
- *Soy allergy:* avoid.
- *Hormone-sensitive cancers:* moderate intake is generally considered safe; very heavy intake of high-isoflavone miso should be discussed with oncology.
- *Renal disease:* moderate due to sodium; verify with nephrology.
- *Hypothyroidism:* the soy-thyroid interaction is generally not significant at typical dietary doses but flagged for awareness.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges from standard Japanese nutritional literature; cardiovascular and cancer-prevention epidemiology from Japanese cohort studies (Tsugane, JPHC Study, multiple publications); sodium considerations from standard nutrition and cardiology guidance. Verify against current cardiology, nephrology, and oncology guidelines for individual patients.]

